   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to the operation of a\nthree-wheeled vehicle",
    "basePrintNo": "S7849",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7849-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§501 & 502, V & T L (as proposed in S.6335-A & A.7192-A)",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7849\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to the opera-\ntion of a three-wheeled vehicle\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to amend the Vehicle and Traffic Law to\nupdate requirements related to the operation of autocycles, three-\nwheeled motor vehicles that are widely available in New York State.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: The bill amends the language authorizing autocycle operation\nwith either a class D driver's license or class M motorcyclist's license\nto clarify that the definition of autocycle includes additional steering\nmechanisms, consistent with various provisions of the Vehicle and Traf-\nfic Law and with national motor vehicle manufacturing standards.\n\nThe existing equipment requirements specified in the 2021 law would\nremain, including seating that does not require the operator to straddle\nor sit astride, seat safety belts for all occupants, and compliance with\nfederal motor vehicle safety standards for motorcycles.\n\nSection 2: Section 2 contains the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York is one of only two remaining states that still require a motor-\ncycle license to operate an autocycle. This bill will provide consistent\nvehicle classification and safe operator licensing with 48 other states.\n{Autocycles are a recent development in the motor vehicle market.} An\nautocycle is a three-wheeled motorcycle that has two wheels situated in\nthe front of the vehicle and one in the rear, a steering wheel, and a\nbucket seat that allows the operator to sit within the vehicle and not\nto straddle or sit astride during operation. Under federal and New York\nstate laws, autocycles are classified as motorcycles because they have\nless than four wheels and have other equipment and safety features\nconsistent with motorcycles.  However, operation of an autocycle is like\nthat of an automobile, since the operator sits in the autocycle, steers\nwith a steering wheel, and uses pedals (gas, brake and clutch) during\noperation of the autocycle.\n\nThe American Association of Motor Vehicle Administrators (AAMVA), along\nwith 48 other states have determined autocycles do not operate as two or\nthree wheel motorcycles, nor do operators employ similar skills to oper-\nate them.\n\nAAMVA has long concluded that attaining a class D license upon a\nsuccessful automotive skills test is an appropriate prerequisite of\noperator. skill prior to operating an autocycle. This bill would elimi-\nnate the need for New York operators to have a motorcycle license, as is\ncurrently required, in order to test drive or otherwise operate an auto-\ncycle. Although a person would not be allowed to take a road test in an\nautocycle, they could operate one with either a class D driver's license\nor class M motorcyclist's license. New York law has been amended over\nthe years to reflect the availability of new types of motor vehicles -\nfrom all-terrain vehicles to limited use automobiles. This bill would\naddress the newest vehicle on the market, the autocycle.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021: Underlying legislation (S.6335-A/A.7192-A passed both houses.\n2020: Previous version (S.4580-C/A.5390-B) vetoed.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNo negative impacts anticipated.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the same date as the underlying chapter,\nwhich will be 120 days after being signed.\n",
